versions.analysis_for_next:
Version 0 got positive and negative recall high, but 130 of 325 neutral texts went to positive (65) or negative (65). Its rule told the model to label overall emotional tone even when the brand is not named, and that contradicts the dataset description. The annotators mark sentiment towards the brand, product or service. Neutral is the large default class.

Neutral texts mislabelled positive include:
- thanks for an answer or fix ('Díky za odpověď', 'Děkuju za vyjasnění');
- hopes and wishes ('snad se v Opavě dočkáme :)', 'Doufám že to projde');
- saying you ordered or own something ('Už mám objednáno =)');
- contest entries and wanting prizes;
- happy chat about one's own life;
- brand promo text;
- praise of things that are not the brand ('Lednice je nádherná').

Neutral texts mislabelled negative include:
- mild regret or sad smileys about circumstances ('nestíháme... :(', 'nám chybí pouze jeden..:((');
- requests and suggestions ('Chci levnější volání...', 'by to chtělo připojení');
- teasing or arguing with other users;
- neutral fault reports and questions with '??';
- dry irony that is not a clear attack on the brand.

Fix: make the target 'opinion about the page's brand, product or service', and make neutral the default when no such clear evaluation exists. Emoticons, thanks, hopes, wishes and requests do not count by themselves. Keep clear complaints (switching provider, overpriced, sarcastic mockery of an offer) and clear praise (loyalty, cute zoo animals, loving the product) as before, since recall on those was strong. Add a few real borderline examples to show where each boundary lies. The previous draft went 1 word over the limit, so this version shortens the wording and drops the Lednice example from neutral, while keeping 'non-brand praise' in the rule.

Version 1 overcorrected. Restricting sentiment to the brand pushed 62 positive texts and 30 negative texts into neutral, and macro-F1 fell from 76.6 to 73.0.

The gold labels show that the annotators do count tone not aimed at the brand.

Positive texts that went to neutral:
- thanks ('Díky za odpověď :-)', 'děkuji za info', 'Děkuji mnohokrát');
- good wishes and congratulations ('všechno nejlepší', 'Držím pěsti!!!', 'krásný den i vám!!!');
- wanting the product ('Dal bych si :P', 'je já chci taky..:)', 'hned bych si dal');
- delight ('krásné sněhové ráno..! :)', 'Jejda mane,to je micánek').

Negative texts that went to neutral:
- sadness or frustration with crying emoticons ('Chudák kočka', 'Nevolááá :((((', 'zase jsem to nestih :-(');
- insults to other users ('ty osle');
- pointed 'why don't you' complaints.

So I return to version 0's emotional-tone framing, which got positive and negative right, and tighten neutral using version 0's errors. A plain smiley or :D on a factual, chatty or joking line is not enough for positive; a feeling word, thanks, wish or desire is needed. Questions, requests, suggestions, wishlists of better tariffs, flat fault reports, banter or teasing jokes, ironic asides without a sad or angry marker, and promo text are neutral. Hope alone ('snad', 'doufám') is neutral unless combined with thanks or delight. Negative needs an explicit complaint, sadness marker, insult or sarcastic attack.

Version 2 scored 79.0. Positive recall is good (142/155) and so is negative (109/120), but 102 neutral texts still go to positive (51) or negative (51).

Neutral texts the model labels positive:
- courtesy thanks closing a question or request ('...jak se dovíme...? Děkuji za info.', 'předem díky za info', 'Díky moc' after asking for points);
- thanks closing an explanation ('Ano myslela jsem to tak... Díky za odpověď');
- contest entries and prize wishes ('mě by se hodily tři', 'Taky potřebuji.. ID', 'dala bych to detičkám');
- descriptive statements about the product or own life with a smiley;
- praise of non-brand places;
- agreement with another user ('Svatá pravda Ondřeji').

Neutral texts the model labels negative:
- factual reports containing 'bohužel', 'škoda' or a single :( ('takže si to bohužel kontrolovat nemůžeme', 'nám chybí pouze jeden..:((', 'Díky za odpověď... nestíháme :(');
- argumentative or ironic replies to other users without a direct insult;
- flat fault reports and questions with ??? or !!!;
- noting a change in taste.

Changes:
- Thanks is positive only when it is the main message; thanks used as a closing formula after a question, request or explanation is neutral.
- Contest or prize requests are neutral.
- 'bohužel' or 'škoda' in a factual line is neutral; negative needs real frustration, a complaint, an insult or a sarcastic attack on the brand.
- Arguing with users without name-calling is neutral.

Keep craving the product ('Dal bych si'), wishes and standalone thanks as positive, so positive recall should hold.

Version 4 brought back version 0's behaviour: positive recall 152/155 and negative 113/120, but 128 neutral texts went to positive or negative, and macro-F1 fell to 77.3.

The broad cues I added caused most of the damage:
- 'cheerful updates with a smiley are positive' (wrong for 'Už je hotový :D', 'akurát pijem :)', 'Praha -Černý most :-)');
- 'disappointment with :( is negative' (wrong for 'nám chybí pouze jeden..:((', 'Proto jsem dnes raději ten internet nevyužívala :-(');
- 'why questions are negative' (wrong for 'Proč je to není dostupne v Polsku?', 'Proč už nefunguje e-shop').

Version 3 had the best balance (80.5), so I return to it. The only change is narrow: in v3, short explicit praise of a product, ad or video ('mě chutnalo ;)', 'je dobrej :-D', 'vtipné') slipped to neutral, so I state that any explicit evaluative word about the product, even mild, makes the text positive. The thanks rule stays as in v3, because loosening it in v4 created about as many errors as it fixed. Smiley-only statements stay neutral. Negative stays limited to real complaints, frustration, crying emoticons, insults and sarcasm, with plain 'why' questions and single-:( factual notes kept neutral.

Version 6 scored 81.8, essentially level with version 5 (81.7). Negative recall rose to 106/120, but neutral→positive grew to 36 and neutral→negative stayed at 42. Neutral, the majority class, still has the weakest recall.

Neutral texts labelled positive:
- promo-like statements about the brand ('slevomat dává možnost zažít stále nové a nové zážitky', 'Kofču na to! ;)', 'Tak tohle žádný slogan nepotřebuje');
- happiness about non-brand things or one's own ideas ('Lednice je nádherná', 'Jsem ráda, že se nápad se jmény chytil');
- plans and visits with a smiley ('no je čas zase navštívit zoo :-)');
- thanks after explanations.

Neutral texts labelled negative:
- ironic or speculative remarks;
- price facts;
- teasing between users.

Many of these get mid probabilities, so I add a tie-breaker: when the feeling is weak, ambiguous or only implied, choose neutral. I also make explicit that promo-style or slogan-like statements about the brand, and joy about non-brand things, are neutral. To stay under the word limit, I swap a neutral example for the slevomat promo line and trim the cue lists.
